315 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 188 0 R
/E (Hunger is the instrument, not the objective. The nafs, when constantly indulged, becomes accustomed to immediate satisfaction, and in that habituation it grows strong, restless, and demanding. Comfort breeds heedlessness, and abundance often conceals inner weakness. When hunger interrupts this cycle, it unsettles the nafs and exposes its impulses; irritation surfaces, impatience emerges, subtle entitlement becomes visible, and the believer begins to witness aspects of the self that remain hidden beneath layers of comfort. Ramadan does not create these tendencies; it unveils them.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
316 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 188 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
317 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 188 0 R
/E (Yet this altered atmosphere does not negate human agency, for the gates may be opened and still be ignored, and mercy may be near and yet be neglected; sacred time intensifies opportunity, but it does not compel response.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
318 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 188 0 R
/E (Ramadan thus stands before the believer as both gift and test, as mercy and as measure, for it reveals whether the heart, when placed in the proximity of divine generosity, inclines toward gratitude and return or toward distraction and delay. To enter Ramadan heedlessly is to misunderstand it; to enter it consciously is to recognize that Allah has invited you into a season of nearness that may not return.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
