372 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 192 0 R
/E (The highest level is the fast of the heart, wherein the heart itself abstains from base thoughts, from excessive attachment to the world, from envy, arrogance, and distraction from Allah. Ibn al-Qayyim described the heart as the sovereign and the limbs as its soldiers, emphasizing that reform of the outward without reform of the inward is incomplete. The heart that fasts from everything that distances it from Allah experiences a refinement that cannot be reduced to physical deprivation. Its hunger becomes clarity. Its thirst becomes insight.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
373 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 192 0 R
/E (At this level, fasting becomes an instrument of purification. It weakens the dominance of the nafs and strengthens the authority of the intellect guided by revelation. It trains the believer to resist impulse and to choose obedience deliberately. It teaches that desire is not an enemy to be destroyed but a force to be disciplined and redirected. Through repeated restraint, the heart begins to loosen its attachment to the transient and incline more naturally toward the eternal. Ramadan thus becomes an ascent. One begins with abstention of the body, progresses to)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
