/T (A Weekly Ramadan Review)
/E (A Weekly Ramadan Review)
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
531 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 203 0 R
/E (Ramadan unfolds quickly, and without structured reflection it can pass with the illusion of progress rather than the reality of change. Establishing a weekly review introduces accountability without harshness and direction without anxiety. At the end of each seven day period, the believer may withdraw briefly from distraction and ask whether tangible internal shifts have begun to occur.)
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
532 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 203 0 R
/E (Has anger decreased in frequency or intensity. Has the tongue become more restrained. Has prayer grown more attentive. Has the attachment to constant comfort weakened even slightly. Has reconciliation been pursued where distance once remained. These questions are not meant to condemn but to illuminate. Awareness precedes correction.)
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
533 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 203 0 R
/E (Writing reflections, even briefly, strengthens sincerity because it externalizes internal assessment. The early scholars feared self deception more than public criticism. They understood that the nafs disguises stagnation as stability and minimal improvement as sufficient. A weekly pause prevents this illusion. It invites the believer to recalibrate mid month rather than regret after its conclusion.)
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
534 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 203 0 R
/E (Such reviews also cultivate gratitude. One may recognize subtle growth that would otherwise go unnoticed. A slightly softened heart, a slightly quicker return to repentance, a slightly stronger resistance to distraction may indicate meaningful progress. Spiritual transformation is often incremental rather than dramatic. The believer who measures gently is more likely to persevere steadily.)
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
